{
  "description": "#5140 Phase 3b — `paste_blocks` parses clipboard text (or takes HTML-paste blocks) and creates the blocks right after the anchor, in one transaction. Covers only what the tauri mock models: an outline whose bullet carries a continuation line and a nested child; two top-level bullets landing in order at the anchor's slot + 1 + k, ahead of the anchor's later siblings; text that does not open with a bullet, read by the same grammar (#5160 Phase 2a: consecutive lines are one paragraph block, a blank line separates blocks, `*` bullets nest by their content column; a heading owning its section is pinned by the mock's own tests, since `#` is a token separator here); a `blocks` input whose two-line block stays ONE block and whose depth-1 entry nests under it; #5160 D4 splices, a paste into the anchor's text (`splice`): mid-block with the first block's child following the anchor's own and `after` ending the last block, a one-block paste that only edits the anchor, `blocks` into an empty block, a first block joined after text as the text it was pasted as (marker, checkbox and property line kept as text, the anchor's own properties untouched), and a last block that is code, after which the text after the cursor is a block of its own; and the refusals. One paste carries page names (#5160 N4): `[[project plan]]` links the seeded `Project Plan` by case, `[[RM]]` links `Roadmap` by its alias, and `[[TWIN]]` ties between `Twin` and `twin` and stays text; a created page's fresh ULID would land in the content where the relabel does not reach, and no content carries `#`, which the command-leg row tokens split on, so creation and the tag rules are pinned by the Rust tests and `reference-tokens.vectors.json` instead; a checkbox after the bullet is the block's `todo_state` on both sides (#5160 D6: in text, in HTML-paste blocks, and in the whole alphabet), while a property line, and a marker after text, both sides keep as text. Another paste carries link labels (#5160 D9): `[[Project Plan|the plan]]` keeps its label as `[[ULID|label]]`, `[[project plan|Project Plan]]` drops one equal to the target's title, and Logseq's `[see it]([[Roadmap]])` is the same token; its `page_links` rows pin that a labelled link is an edge. The returns pin every created row in document order. Seed: S1 (Home) > S2 (the anchor), S3, S4 > S5 (purged by an op), S6 > S7, S8 (empty), S9, S10, S11 (Project Plan), S12 (Roadmap, alias `rm` set by an op), S13 (Twin), S14 (twin). Re-author `expected` / `expected_ops` via CONFORMANCE_UPDATE=1.",
